---
import CodeWindow from "../components/CodeWindow.astro";
import MainThread from "../components/MainThread.astro";
import PNotes from "../components/PNotes.astro";
---

<p-slide autoplay timing="800">
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Remember our heavy task? <span aria-hidden="true">🤔</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow is:raw file="heavy-task.js" lang="js">
    const heavyTask = () => {
      for (let i = 0; i < 10000000000; i++) {
        //do something useful
      }
    };
  </CodeWindow>
  <p-fragment timing-start="1200">
    <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-start: 0.5em">
      Cute. <span gradient="sub">Now meet the real one</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true">🤖</span>
    </h4>
  </p-fragment>
  <PNotes>
    <p>Callback to the opening: the fake for loop was the tutorial version.</p>
    <p>
      In 2026 the heavy task your users will actually hit is AI inference
      running locally in the browser.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span aria-hidden="true">🤗</span> <span gradient>transformers.js</span>
  </h3>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🧠 <span gradient="sub">Hugging Face models</span> directly in the browser
      — no server
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🔌 Runs on <span gradient="sub">ONNX Runtime</span> — WASM (CPU) or WebGPU
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      📦 Model downloaded once, then
      <span gradient="sub"> cached by the browser</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      ✈️ Works <span gradient="sub">offline</span> — no API keys, no per-token
      billing
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>Official Hugging Face library, mirrors the Python transformers API.</p>
    <p>
      v4 (early 2026) ships a new WebGPU runtime written in C++ with the ONNX
      Runtime team. Current stable on npm: 4.3.x (plain npm install).
    </p>
    <p>
      Models are ONNX conversions, thousands ready on the Hub. Quantized
      sentiment model ≈ 65MB.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow is:raw file="sentiment.js" lang="js" fragments="5, 7">
    import { pipeline } from '@huggingface/transformers';

    const classifier = await pipeline('sentiment-analysis');

    const result = await classifier('I love web workers!');

    // [{ label: 'POSITIVE', score: 0.9998 }]
  </CodeWindow>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      pipeline() does everything: download, cache, tokenization, inference,
      post-processing.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tasks: sentiment, embeddings, translation, Whisper speech-to-text, image
      classification…
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span aria-hidden="true">📍</span>{" "}
    <span gradient>But where does it run?</span>
  </h3>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🧮 <span gradient="sub">WASM backend</span>: inference runs on
      <b>the thread that calls it</b>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      💀 Call it from the main thread →
      <span gradient="sub">it IS heavyTask()</span>, for real
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      ⚡ <span gradient="sub">WebGPU</span> helps, but tokenization &amp;
      post-processing stay on CPU
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      📣 The official HF examples run the pipeline
      <span gradient="sub"> inside a Web Worker</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <MainThread mood="💀" line="a whole AI model… on ME?!" frozen="12,000 ms" />
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      This is the bridge: not my personal hack, it's the canonical pattern of
      the library.
    </p>
    <p>
      Nuance if asked: ONNX Runtime can use extra WASM threads, but only on
      cross-origin isolated pages (SharedArrayBuffer), which most apps are not.
      By default the work runs on the calling thread.
    </p>
    <p>
      WebGPU is now in Chrome/Edge, Safari 26 and Firefox (Windows), but mobile
      and older devices still fall back to WASM on the CPU, so the blocking path
      is one many users will hit.
    </p>
    <p>Last click: the Main Thread is back, counter going up.</p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h4 layout="center" style="margin-block-end: 0.5em">
    Let's Code <span aria-hidden="true">👨‍💻</span>
  </h4>
  <CodeWindow is:raw file="ai.worker.ts" lang="ts" lines={7} fragments="10-14">
    /// <reference lib="webworker" />
    import { pipeline } from '@huggingface/transformers';

    const loading = pipeline( // a Promise: the model loads once
      'sentiment-analysis',
      'Xenova/distilbert-base-uncased-finetuned-sst-2-english',
      { dtype: 'q8' }
    );

    addEventListener('message', async ({ data }) => {
      const classifier = await loading;
      const result = await classifier(data); // 🤖 the REAL heavy task
      postMessage(result);
    });
  </CodeWindow>
  <p
    p-fragment
    p-effect="from below"
    layout="center"
    text="mid"
    style="margin-block-start: 0.5em"
  >
    😏 Same worker, same <code>postMessage</code>, same
    <span gradient="sub">resource()</span>
  </p>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Structurally identical to worker-test.worker.ts from earlier — only
      heavyTask() changed.
    </p>
    <p>
      No top-level await on purpose: the pipeline Promise is created once and
      awaited in the handler, so it works in classic and module workers alike
      (it's also the pattern of the official HF worker examples).
    </p>
    <p>
      In Angular: prompt in a signal → resource() → this worker → isLoading()
      while the model crunches. Zero new architecture.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <iframe
    src="/demo/ai-live-demo.html"
    title="AI inference live demo: main thread vs worker"
    style="display: block; inline-size: 92%; aspect-ratio: 16 / 9; margin-inline: auto; border: 1px solid var(--line-color); border-radius: 0.3em; background: white"
  ></iframe>
  <p-fragment iframe-key="1" p-effect="none"></p-fragment>
  <p-fragment iframe-key="2" p-effect="none"></p-fragment>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Just press next, also from the speaker view: the deck presses the demo
      keys in the presentation window (never in the speaker view, so notes and
      timer don't freeze). Open the page before the talk so the model is cached.
    </p>
    <p>
      Next #1 → [2] worker: the ball keeps moving. Next #2 → [1] main: freeze.
    </p>
    <p>
      While main runs, spam the "click me" button: clicks replay all at once
      when the thread frees up.
    </p>
    <p>
      The ball is rAF-driven on purpose: a CSS animation runs on the compositor
      and would keep moving even with a blocked main thread.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you click inside the iframe, click outside it again before using the
      arrow keys: keyboard focus inside the iframe doesn't reach the deck.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>

<p-slide>
  <h3>
    <span aria-hidden="true">🎒</span> <span gradient>Good to know</span>
  </h3>
  <ul density="spaced" text="mid">
    <li p-fragment p-effect="zoom">
      📥 Big first download: <span gradient="sub">show progress</span>
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from right">
      🎚️ <span gradient="sub">Quantization</span> (<code>q8</code>,{" "}
      <code>q4</code>): smaller and lighter
    </li>
    <li p-fragment p-effect="from left">
      🚚 Big outputs (embeddings)? <span gradient="sub">Transfer</span> them
    </li>
  </ul>
  <PNotes>
    <p>
      Progress: pass progress_callback to pipeline(). Quantization: dtype: 'q8'
      / 'q4' → smaller download, less RAM.
    </p>
    <p>
      Transfer: sentiment returns a tiny plain object, nothing to gain. But an
      embeddings pipeline returns a Tensor whose .data is a Float32Array: post
      it with [output.data.buffer] in the transfer list instead of cloning it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Pre-cache tip: load the pipeline once on app bootstrap (or a service
      worker) so users never wait on click.
    </p>
    <p>
      The transfer bullet ties back to the postMessage(message, transfer) slide
      — this is where it stops being theoretical.
    </p>
    <p>
      Close the section by pointing at Davide's talk: I show WHERE this stuff
      must run, he shows WHAT LLMs can do with it.
    </p>
  </PNotes>
</p-slide>
